---
/// <reference path="../../sql.d.ts" />
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { isAuthenticated } from '../../lib/adminAuth.mjs';
import auditTemplate from '../../../public/sql/configure-auditing.sql?raw';

if (!isAuthenticated(Astro.cookies)) {
  return Astro.redirect('/admin/login');
}
---
<Layout title="SQL auditing">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Security controls</p>
    <h1>SQL auditing configuration</h1>
    <p>SQL Server Audit configuration for database, schema, and table or view activity, with optional server authentication and security events.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>What this scenario enables</h2>
      <p>Applied by the VM bootstrap</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Audit target</div><div class="value">Windows Application log</div><p>SQL Server Audit writes to <code>APPLICATION_LOG</code>, which AMA forwards through the <code>Event</code> table.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Data changes</div><div class="value">SELECT · INSERT · UPDATE · DELETE</div><p><code>FutonManufacturingDbAuditSpec</code> tracks database-level activity by <code>public</code>.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Server activity</div><div class="value">Logins and security changes</div><p>Login success/failure, password changes, principal changes, permission changes, role membership, and audit changes.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Forwarding</div><div class="value">AMA + DCR</div><p>The VM's Windows Application log is forwarded to the configured Sentinel workspace for queries and detections.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Reusable SQL Server audit script</h2>
      <p>Run as a controlled sysadmin operation</p>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/sql/configure-auditing.sql" download>Download audit configuration SQL</a></p>
    <p>Set the variables at the top for an existing database and choose <code>DATABASE</code>, <code>SCHEMA</code>, or <code>OBJECT</code> scope. OBJECT accepts a table or view, with schema and object names supplied separately. Choose the data actions and an existing database user or role; <code>public</code> includes all database users. Server activity is optional and instance-wide, not limited by the database scope.</p>
    <p>The default <code>PREVIEW</code> mode validates the selected targets and returns setup and verification SQL without writing configuration. Use <code>APPLY</code> after reviewing the generated SQL, or <code>VERIFY</code> to inspect existing audit definitions and runtime state. Existing audit names are never overwritten. A failed apply can leave partially created definitions; inspect them before retrying.</p>
    <p>Supports SQL Server on Windows or Linux, including SQL Server VMs. FILE requires an existing directory on the server writable by the SQL Server service account. Set a Windows path such as <code>C:\SQLAudit\</code> or a Linux path. APPLICATION_LOG is Windows-only. Azure SQL Database, Azure SQL Managed Instance, Fabric, and other database engines require their own audit configuration; this script rejects those platforms.</p>
    <pre><code>{auditTemplate}</code></pre>
    <p class="warn">Audit-only: this script does not restore databases, change credentials, grant permissions, or configure log forwarding. Run with authorized SQL audit-management permissions: ALTER ANY SERVER AUDIT and ALTER ANY DATABASE AUDIT, plus database access and permissions to read the verification catalog views and DMVs. Review privacy, event volume, and retention: FILE uses 100 MB files and 10 rollover files, deleting the oldest files as it rolls over. ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE favors availability and can allow unaudited operations during an audit-target failure.</p>
    <p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-server-audit-transact-sql" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Server audit reference</a> · <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-database-audit-specification-transact-sql" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Database audit scope reference</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Verify the controls</h2>
      <p>Use VERIFY mode with the same audit names</p>
    </div>
    <p>The script reports audit enablement, target, failure policy, runtime status, selected action groups, and database scope IDs. Confirm runtime status is STARTED and inspect actual audit records separately; configured specifications alone do not prove event delivery. FILE records can be read with <code>sys.fn_get_audit_file</code>. The query below applies only to Windows Application-log collection into Log Analytics.</p>
  <h3>Log Analytics verification (KQL, not T-SQL)</h3>
  <pre><code>Event
| where TimeGenerated &gt; ago(30m)
| where EventLog == 'Application'
| where Source == 'MSSQLSERVER' or Source startswith 'MSSQL$'
| where EventID == 33205
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, EventID, RenderedDescription
| order by TimeGenerated desc
| take 50</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Coverage and limits</h2>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>Event ID 33205 contains SQL Server Audit action details, including data-change operations.</li>
      <li>Event IDs 18453, 18454, and 18456 supplement successful and failed login visibility.</li>
      <li>The admin page independently confirms operator actions through the forwarded Windows event stream.</li>
      <li>Audit success does not prove Key Vault synchronization; those are separate checks.</li>
      <li>SQL Audit records statements and principals, not a full row before/after history or proof of transaction commit. Use temporal tables or change data capture when row history is required.</li>
      <li>QUEUE_DELAY = 1000 queues delivery for up to roughly one second. ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE favors availability and can permit operations without an audit record during a target failure.</li>
      <li>Application-log collection is suitable for this training scenario; production workloads may need a dedicated audit file target, retention policy, and narrower object scope.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>